    {
      "id": "cQ5nE_jgYPU0Pf8B3Ibnw",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 289.8075277268703,
      "y": 819.9088393828413,
      "width": 1056.9991455078125,
      "height": 825,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0a",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 816150253,
      "version": 1760,
      "versionNonce": 1807055107,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1782168460654,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "*** Rendering approach:\n1. DOM\n-- Construct drawing directly in DOM, relies heavily in browsers' layout and painting engine,\ncan leverage CSS layout, resolution independent and scale in any zoom\n-- Built-in event handling (eg: Webflow lean heavily on DOM-based rendering) \n-- Cons: though simple, it create overhead on layout tree, performance deteriorates as number\nof elements grow\n2. SVG\n-- Vector-based, resolution independent and scale at any zoom\n-- Declarative markup is easy to read and manipulate\n-- Individual shapes are addressable, enabling straightforward hit-testing and interactivity\n-- Cons:\n---- DOM overhead makes it slows with thousands of elements\n---- less suitable for freehand drawing \n3. Canvas\n-- Developers draw into a bitmap using JavaScript operations such as ctx.fillRect() or ctx.arc(). \n-- High performance for hundreds to thousands of shapes\n-- support freehand drawing and pixel-level operations\n-- fine-grained control over rendering\n-- Cons:\n--- text rendering and editing are difficult\n--- flex layout does not exist\n-- No retained mode --> must reimplement its own scene graph\n-- canvas backing store is a bitmap, so naive zooming via css transform will be blurry, zoom requie redraw\n4. WebGL (Figma choice) -- rendering with GPU\n-- Hardware acceleration supports massive scale and complex effects\n-- Superior performance compared to DOM, SVG, and Canvas\n-- Flexible enough to render both 2D and 3D\n-- Cons\n---- Shares Canvas's limitations, since text rendering and editing are difficult and flex layout does not exist\n---- Steep learning curve and verbose setup\n---- Requires building or adopting an engine for scene management\n---- Debugging is difficult compared to higher-level approaches",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "*** Rendering approach:\n1. DOM\n-- Construct drawing directly in DOM, relies heavily in browsers' layout and painting engine,\ncan leverage CSS layout, resolution independent and scale in any zoom\n-- Built-in event handling (eg: Webflow lean heavily on DOM-based rendering) \n-- Cons: though simple, it create overhead on layout tree, performance deteriorates as number\nof elements grow\n2. SVG\n-- Vector-based, resolution independent and scale at any zoom\n-- Declarative markup is easy to read and manipulate\n-- Individual shapes are addressable, enabling straightforward hit-testing and interactivity\n-- Cons:\n---- DOM overhead makes it slows with thousands of elements\n---- less suitable for freehand drawing \n3. Canvas\n-- Developers draw into a bitmap using JavaScript operations such as ctx.fillRect() or ctx.arc(). \n-- High performance for hundreds to thousands of shapes\n-- support freehand drawing and pixel-level operations\n-- fine-grained control over rendering\n-- Cons:\n--- text rendering and editing are difficult\n--- flex layout does not exist\n-- No retained mode --> must reimplement its own scene graph\n-- canvas backing store is a bitmap, so naive zooming via css transform will be blurry, zoom requie redraw\n4. WebGL (Figma choice) -- rendering with GPU\n-- Hardware acceleration supports massive scale and complex effects\n-- Superior performance compared to DOM, SVG, and Canvas\n-- Flexible enough to render both 2D and 3D\n-- Cons\n---- Shares Canvas's limitations, since text rendering and editing are difficult and flex layout does not exist\n---- Steep learning curve and verbose setup\n---- Requires building or adopting an engine for scene management\n---- Debugging is difficult compared to higher-level approaches",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
